# Python C3 S3-C implementation findings

Worktree: /Users/sto/workspace/datomworld-s3c
Branch: yang-python-c3-s3c, stacked on S3-B. No git writes.

## Implementation

- &, |, "'^ use integer/bit-and, bit-or, bit-xor, wrapped by
  py/int-result. Two bool operands return a bool; mixed bool/int returns
  an int. ~ uses integer/bit-not. Guest types are checked first.
- << and >> share py/shift: type validation precedes integer/compare'"'s
  negative-count precheck, which raises ValueError with exactly
  \"negative shift count\". The corresponding module kernel runs only
  after validation, wrapped by py/int-result. Zero, huge counts, count
  zero, negative right-shift floor and bit-limit failures are pinned.
- Both floaty power paths precheck abs(e) >= 2"'^1024 and raise the exact
  OverflowError message. The implementation compares abs(e) >> 1024
  against zero through integer/compare: mathematically equivalent, and
  avoids constructing a 1025-bit threshold under a smaller profile.
  This is the sole implementation variation from a literal comparison.
- Header now describes exact module integer operations. Float floor'"'s
  existing supported range remains separate.
- py/int-of uses integer/from-float, wrapped by py/int-result.
  py/float-parts-down compares the reconstructed safe integer converted
  back to double, then doubles exactly until integral. Float parts up,
  rational key reduction and hash arithmetic are unchanged.
- py/key and py/hash handle zero directly, retaining the existing raw
  host -0 compatibility pins. No canonicalization helper is needed.

## Helper deletion audit

Deleted all twelve requested helpers: py/checked-add, py/checked-sub,
py/checked-mul, py/overflow, py/ipow, py/int-floordiv, py/int-mod,
py/bit1, py/bit-op, py/shift-check, py/divmod-pos, py/int-canon.
No helper from that list remains. Search finds no remaining reference.
Shared reconstruction helpers py/int-of, py/float-parts-up/down and
py/float-parts remain live in float key/hash reconstruction and power.
py/int-op is retained as typed module dispatch, not bounded arithmetic.

## Tests and fixture

- int-ops-v3.txt: 1,396 measured CPython 3.9.6 rows, generated by the
  adjacent script with an asserted interpreter version and explicit
  binary, shift and unary operator lists. Regeneration is byte-identical.
- Includes big/negative operands, all bool combinations, count zero,
  52/53/54/63/64/100/127 counts, huge negative counts, huge right counts
  and zero shifted left by huge positive counts. Huge nonzero left
  allocation failures are profile-specific fast pins, not allocations
  in the generator.
- All rows use 100-row programs on each VM, and 64-row parser programs.
  The portable slow test has both "'^:slow and dao.test-slow/guard.
  Frozen v1/v2 fixtures and their generators are unchanged.
- New fast tests: exact-bitwise-shifts-test, shift-bit-limit-test and
  float-power-exponent-limit-test. Float expectations use host-floats.
- Parser augmented test pins all five augmented operators and one
  index evaluation each, plus the bool result type through printed text.
- Restored prelude parity'"'s 1<<53 row, using its existing exact-literals
  and canon-ints comparison; added 1<<54 and 0<<100 rows. Restored actual
  shift/invert expressions in integer-bound pins in parity/e2e-c1.
- Existing numeric key/hash tests retain all their expected values,
  including subnormals, maximum finite binary64, rational reduction,
  bool/int/float key identity, adjacent big integers and raw -0.
- Re-minted the five prelude-dependent float-address goldens once from
  final source; hook prelude and other content goldens stay unchanged.

## Red, green and mutations

Initial focused int-ops run: 13 tests, 49 assertions, 12 failures and
zero errors. Each new test failed on all four JVM VMs with old helpers.
After implementation: same 13 tests and 49 assertions, zero failures
and errors (before adding the v3 slow corpus test).

Six deliberate mutations, each producing four failed assertions (one
per VM), zero errors; restored automatically with with-redefs:

1. Return int for bool & bool instead of bool.
2. Apply ~ to abs(a), losing a negative operand's sign.
3. Apply & to abs(a), losing negative two's-complement sign bits.
4. Drop negative-count precheck, exposing the module refusal.
5. Truncate a negative right shift instead of taking floor.
6. Change count zero to one.

Logs: target/s3c-red.log, target/s3c-green.log and
 target/s3c-mutations.log; mutation runner target/s3c-mutations.clj.

